n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $-\\frac{27}{3}$ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply." "To which of the following sets does the number $-\\frac{27}{3}$ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186470186 :answer-field/key "C" "Complex numbers ($C$)"] [:db/cas 17592186470187 :answer-field/key "N" "Natural numbers ($N$)"] [:db/cas 17592186470188 :answer-field/key "Q" "Rational numbers ($Q$)"] [:db/cas 17592186470189 :answer-field/key "R" "Real numbers ($R$)"] [:db/cas 17592186470190 :answer-field/key "Z" "Integers ($Z$)"] [:db/cas 17592186348365 :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nN,\\,Z,\\,Q,\\,R,\\,C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $\\pi $ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply." "To which of the following sets does the number $\\pi $ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186470191 :answer-field/key "C" "Complex numbers ($C$)"] [:db/cas 17592186470192 :answer-field/key "N" "Natural numbers ($N$)"] [:db/cas 17592186470193 :answer-field/key "Q" "Rational numbers ($Q$)"] [:db/cas 17592186470194 :answer-field/key "R" "Real numbers ($R$)"] [:db/cas 17592186470195 :answer-field/key "Z" "Integers ($Z$)"] [:db/cas 17592186377893 :question/problem "According to the Chinese remainder theorem, which of the following systems of congruences has a unique solution modulo $48?$ \n\n$$\n\\text{I.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{II.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡14 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{III.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "According to the Chinese remainder theorem, which of the following systems of congruences has a unique solution modulo $48?$ \n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186377893 :question/worked-solution "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers ${n}_{1},{n}_{2},…,{n}_{k}$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that $\\text{gcd}({n}_{i},{n}_{j})=1$ whenever $i\\ne j,$ then for any integers ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{k}$ the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x≡{a}_{1} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{1}) \\\\ x≡{a}_{2} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{2}) \\\\ \\,⋮ \\\\ x≡{a}_{k} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo ${n}_{1}{n}_{2}⋯{n}_{k}.$\n\nLet's now inspect each of the given systems in turn.\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system I since $6$ and $8$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(6,8)=2>1\n$$\n\n- The theorem applies to system II. The numbers $3,$ and $16$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $3\\cdot 16=48.$\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system III since $4$ and $12$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(4,12)=4>1\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers ${n}_{1},{n}_{2},…,{n}_{k}$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that $\\text{gcd}({n}_{i},{n}_{j})=1$ whenever $i\\ne j,$ then for any integers ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{k}$ the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡{a}_{1} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{1}) \\\\ x≡{a}_{2} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{2}) \\\\ \\,⋮ \\\\ x≡{a}_{k} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{k})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo ${n}_{1}{n}_{2}⋯{n}_{k}.$\n\nLet's now inspect each of the given systems in turn.\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ since $6$ and $8$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(6,8)=2>1\n$$\n\n- The theorem applies to system $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡14 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\right.$. The numbers $3,$ and $16$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $3\\cdot 16=48.$\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ since $4$ and $12$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(4,12)=4>1\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡14 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186472388 :answer-field/key "I" "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\\right.$"] [:db/cas 17592186472389 :answer-field/key "II" "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡14 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\right.$"] [:db/cas 17592186472390 :answer-field/key "III" "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\\right.$"] [:db/cas 17592186381826 :question/problem "$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}y=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,x{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}>3\\,)\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." "$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,{x}y=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}\\in (1,2),\\,x{z}>3\\,)\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186381826 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $x$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $z$ is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,xy=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,xz>3\\,)\n$$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $z$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∀$ that uses $z:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,xy=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀z\\in (1,2),\\,\\overbrace{x{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>3}^{\\text{scope of }∀z})\n$$\n\n \n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∃{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>3\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"I only.\"" "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- The occurrence of $x$ in $xy=1$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- The occurrence of $z$ immediately after $∀$ is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,xy=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,xz>3\\,)\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $z$ in $xz>3$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∀$ that uses $z:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,xy=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀z\\in (1,2),\\,\\overbrace{x{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>3}^{\\text{scope of }∀z})\n$$\n\n \n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∃{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>3\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is “The occurrence of $x$ in $xy=1$” only"] [:db/cas 17592186472158 :answer-field/key "I" "The occurrence of $x$ in $xy=1$"] [:db/cas 17592186472159 :answer-field/key "II" "The occurrence of $z$ immediately after $∀$"] [:db/cas 17592186472160 :answer-field/key "III" "The occurrence of $z$ in $xz>3$"] [:db/cas 17592186381867 :question/problem "$$\n∀{\\,y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,y+{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}\\le {\\,z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." "$$\n∀{\\,y}\\in Z,\\,y+{x}\\le {\\,z}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186381867 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $y$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n∀{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,y+x\\le z\n$$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $x$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $z$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $z.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∀{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,y+{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\\le {z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrences are \"II and III only.\"" "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- The occurrence of $y$ immediately after $∀$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n∀{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,y+x\\le z\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $x$ in $y+x\\le z$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- The occurrence of $z$ on the right side of $y+x\\le z$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $z.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∀{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,y+{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\\le {z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrences are “The occurrence of $x$ in $y+x\\le z$”; “The occurrence of $z$ on the right side of $y+x\\le z$” only"] [:db/cas 17592186472185 :answer-field/key "I" "The occurrence of $y$ immediately after $∀$"] [:db/cas 17592186472186 :answer-field/key "II" "The occurrence of $x$ in $y+x\\le z$"] [:db/cas 17592186472187 :answer-field/key "III" "The occurrence of $z$ on the right side of $y+x\\le z$"] [:db/cas 17592186381874 :question/problem "$$\n(\\,∀x\\in N,\\,x>{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃z\\in R,\\,{{z}^{3}}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." "$$\n(\\,∀x\\in N,\\,x>{n}+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃z\\in R,\\,{{z}^{3}}-1={y}\\,)\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186381874 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $n$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $n.$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $z$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃z$ that uses $z:$ \n\n$$\n(\\,∀x\\in N,\\,x>n+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃z\\in R,\\,\\overbrace{{z}^{3}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}^{\\text{scope of }∃z}\\,)\n$$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $y$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $y.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in N,\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,{{z}^{3}}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"I and III only.\"" "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- The occurrence of $n$ in $x>n+1$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $n.$\n\n- The occurrence of $z$ in ${z}^{3}-1=y$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃z$ that uses $z:$ \n\n$$\n(\\,∀x\\in N,\\,x>n+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃z\\in R,\\,\\overbrace{{z}^{3}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}^{\\text{scope of }∃z}\\,)\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $y$ on the right side of ${z}^{3}-1=y$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $y.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in N,\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,{{z}^{3}}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is “The occurrence of $n$ in $x>n+1$”; “The occurrence of $y$ on the right side of ${z}^{3}-1=y$” only"] [:db/cas 17592186472188 :answer-field/key "I" "The occurrence of $n$ in $x>n+1$"] [:db/cas 17592186472189 :answer-field/key "II" "The occurrence of $z$ in ${z}^{3}-1=y$"] [:db/cas 17592186472190 :answer-field/key "III" "The occurrence of $y$ on the right side of ${z}^{3}-1=y$"] [:db/cas 17592186390205 :question/problem "$$\n∃{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}={w}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." "$$\n∃{x}\\in R,\\,2-{x}={w}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186390205 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $x$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∃:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-x=w\n$$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $x$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃x$ that uses $x:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,x\\in R,\\,\\overbrace{2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}=w}^{\\text{scope of }∃x}\n$$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $w$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $w.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∃{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}={w}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"III only.\"" "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- The occurrence of $x$ immediately after $∃$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∃:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-x=w\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $x$ in $2-x=w$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃x$ that uses $x:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,x\\in R,\\,\\overbrace{2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}=w}^{\\text{scope of }∃x}\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $w$ on the right side of $2-x=w$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $w.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∃{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}={w}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is “The occurrence of $w$ on the right side of $2-x=w$” only"] [:db/cas 17592186472152 :answer-field/key "I" "The occurrence of $x$ immediately after $∃$"] [:db/cas 17592186472153 :answer-field/key "II" "The occurrence of $x$ in $2-x=w$"] [:db/cas 17592186472154 :answer-field/key "III" "The occurrence of $w$ on the right side of $2-x=w$"] [:db/cas 17592186390212 :question/problem "$$\n∃\\,\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}\\,|\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above 