g)"] [:db/cas 17592186440185 :answer-field/key "III" "![Graph III](images/3c/3c01cf271870556b9e6870ac7c814c05496c010a7b7e8ebb7b16905188af5df2.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186440186 :answer-field/key "IV" "![Graph IV](images/46/4651b8b75b33ee763d138713d25e4255b607851f1fd53cdbbf2de2e89a82f590.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186172664 :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b0ced561cd98f9cf81c7de42bee7411a39595a9c59f2edaad0121d712dad157.png)\n\nWhich of the following curves is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following curves is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186172664 :question/worked-solution "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented.\n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve II is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve III is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve IV is negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented.\n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve II is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve III is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve IV is negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is curves I and IV only"] [:db/cas 17592186461112 :answer-field/key "I" "![Curve I](images/33/331d09701db83dfc483e5792326e0bd8fb027fb014ee1a5ade5d4f5a0511bc0b.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186461113 :answer-field/key "II" "![Curve II](images/43/43850698422bba70759c1596d5cff531012480fb7e2c6828e4d811e8271aa2b3.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186461114 :answer-field/key "III" "![Curve III](images/ee/eee6911fd5099f8b8f2246d12abccc86183aad82858e4a06d3636163262cac2e.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186461115 :answer-field/key "IV" "![Curve IV](images/1d/1d45e88e15b95a0aa156c9ce28c20e07366f556a7d0fcd39dd8a369b384fc783.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186172669 :question/problem "![](images/00/0045eda99f40f774d2ef21214522eebb0c53bffee304ba503a9076a7491cc648.png)\n\nWhich of the regions shown above are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the regions shown below are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186172669 :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions: \n\n- Region I is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region II is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains two holes.\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is not simply connected. Instead, this is a region that consists of two pieces (it's not connected).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions: \n\n- Region I is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region II is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains two holes.\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is not simply connected. Instead, this is a region that consists of two pieces (it's not connected).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is regions I and III only"] [:db/cas 17592186461116 :answer-field/key "I" "![Region I](images/a1/a10ff6f49ddef971e1d0903606f7ec7e9854ca61f54b06628de30cc43f9eb084.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186461117 :answer-field/key "II" "![Region II](images/85/85cb1a2ef9bbf5730f45c16debc2907e0c1523da902d288382702e88135cc5fb.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186461118 :answer-field/key "III" "![Region III](images/55/55e389798da7756773eaa9421db393ef683918a4e188748f25e3f3ae589aaf7b.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186461119 :answer-field/key "IV" "![Region IV](images/76/7637f501aa3827e740acd7e6760f2ecdce85b8fbf6cfa0db69e3615751909927.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186193312 :question/problem "![](images/eb/ebeebae27d24ae4dd342b9cf65f93fe58e77aa8dcd99a7cde0880f68378862fa.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186460772 :answer-field/key "I" "![Diagram I](images/e7/e79e96039bc2ebbe1787345b90a84e42e7410c0ef500dfac37844bdc1a10e756.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186460773 :answer-field/key "II" "![Diagram II](images/76/7655da5d10b7ace907bd613342d576a691451532aaa7564a9e17c1b41a66097b.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186460774 :answer-field/key "III" "![Diagram III](images/f0/f04fb553eed6853549b0aa6c0f03b05e7f69931d8ba1cfaf0cef2486c82820d7.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186193319 :question/problem "![](images/df/df3b62554600601401d14d3b6758a3c350cf8cddc3788e79a3ba456a5a19c60a.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186193319 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram II only"] [:db/cas 17592186460790 :answer-field/key "I" "![Diagram I](images/6e/6e8c180b3963c096afadfcfe590f8cff8ee3859438aab883ef08fa18d35ed503.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186460791 :answer-field/key "II" "![Diagram II](images/cd/cdc289944ccb8df5531321f0cea6f1cd9e81cc7f194ee13d0db078c47471696a.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186460792 :answer-field/key "III" "![Diagram III](images/90/902a485b7c5d2c3fd940b06123ee5316fb74b5e2a2d969b7bf0562fb15cf1a58.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186204256 :question/problem "![](images/fd/fdbcfe164fcca0e005255de10f10c10bedc156f3a582eeb5800322d4c4ebe0ab.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent symmetric relations defined on the set $A={0,2,4}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the plots below represent symmetric relations defined on the set $A={0,2,4}?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186204256 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/9c/9c9c16300cfb95a61a261e39db865d5c6d056794bd7aaa422567bde10dd4abf5.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(4,2)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(2,4)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(0,4)$ and $(4,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/9c/9c9c16300cfb95a61a261e39db865d5c6d056794bd7aaa422567bde10dd4abf5.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(4,2)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(2,4)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(0,4)$ and $(4,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is plot I only"] [:db/cas 17592186462872 :answer-field/key "I" "![Plot I](images/72/72ce19aec6251ebe580f3b3c2368c1d56d020d547c4e2fb78b33cf3bfc6f9e9e.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186462873 :answer-field/key "II" "![Plot II](images/ca/cabd107b8ba19060094506c0e58e37ae1e936f49e4ff93f7ca81acbc81fe46b6.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186462874 :answer-field/key "III" "![Plot III](images/0c/0cbce0239db07380cd4ee60451a550c40edc82f1c7bba6e4f90a65091d289e7f.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186244143 :question/problem "![](images/89/89b0be0ca7ae1a6e76d32bfc669d6f554deaaad2055b2875e3f6a49821043205.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186438788 :answer-field/key "I" "![Graph I](images/74/749427225b10ed6998d384640273a2583f58ec596bb8ac9f006f53662e62850a.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186438789 :answer-field/key "II" "![Graph II](images/86/86abd8cebd946cacb0f6e6b5c36cf9292a3c5d99e471822307b0638377949b95.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186438790 :answer-field/key "III" "![Graph III](images/45/45c8195e54ab766f50439d63146e299f0602766b6c3d7c37942282db6e578185.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186438791 :answer-field/key "IV" "![Graph IV](images/26/26893dd5faa2990bc8bfd53602863ee15fbad8008c52c59a3abd19b960904c70.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186244144 :question/problem "![](images/a6/a6e311063c3222d8be1b5fe37379d21c4dbb3198afd7c338a273bb1cf366fc1e.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186438792 :answer-field/key "I" "![Graph I](images/9b/9beab54a4175ffdc30be00f3465c14