on?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186439220 :answer-field/key "I" "![Graph I](images/28/28f275fb7c96fca9f1507c662f3a6f8a9206fdcf02c7c68374c3dc36b421d49b.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186439221 :answer-field/key "II" "![Graph II](images/87/8768075443ee9b3baba837a2d7fda447b494bb8f4a6d04147c1a001b3d3ce8dd.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186439222 :answer-field/key "III" "![Graph III](images/19/198e04631ba8014a185bc0b93722e4e7f2a177ff9a8ecd9f45d71dce65a5f640.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186439223 :answer-field/key "IV" "![Graph IV](images/e5/e5ec0646de66b31dd229a85fd8c3db7ee581de8f013819b5f41716dec2854cb8.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186403251 :question/problem "![](images/f6/f65062d48189401ba22b1b08637d187eed532d5f93dca7663c2e80743510d5d7.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186439228 :answer-field/key "I" "![Graph I](images/97/974f37046e173f481308b82f273c4b223b985627946e32c34212e43a9f9ed907.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186439229 :answer-field/key "II" "![Graph II](images/e6/e64baa6361ff355e5efe450a286a7bde5303bf182b2894c6d3e461b9345c6992.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186439230 :answer-field/key "III" "![Graph III](images/e7/e78c2b984ba0a1fecc3fa1538b3c4b4e8746ab69808dc0378e8732fec5dfe557.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186439231 :answer-field/key "IV" "![Graph IV](images/b3/b3bec6fb593585817ac70d38a02f278c2b6faa13cd11d8ae4785fe9a815a344f.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186407336 :question/problem "![](images/42/42e0a9a9e987c1c56a335f29cf939e4f03d496fad148f148c32377893dbe7c13.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186407336 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.  \n\n- Diagram I is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.  \n\n- Diagram I is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram I only."] [:db/cas 17592186474052 :answer-field/key "I" "![Diagram I](images/94/9484c41b7b3141c697d25e72b106f018cb78f2bc68f2d671555c8d347f37f726.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186474053 :answer-field/key "II" "![Diagram II](images/e7/e7d5fdbccc6e9fc256924810e9fb7867fb30a52edad837a7dbb55c3fee857901.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186474054 :answer-field/key "III" "![Diagram III](images/cf/cf2db417b6f90899d1264ecb763f90c3bb654e37be4abc4221e912b2a4dc09ee.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186407337 :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d5d9586684c8606ca0f6df8dd70d9828ea04d3d9097b5a155ab66bcd7300ffd.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186473939 :answer-field/key "I" "![Diagram I](images/53/5303e5de04148fbd414db26930d50d5c0cd4ef19af5a46afaf4b312966cdfded.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186473940 :answer-field/key "II" "![Diagram II](images/cc/ccdca51aef8ee61c23d3f3a81b1e4673598ffdde88cb4f58b4b35bb76886f524.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186473941 :answer-field/key "III" "![Diagram III](images/e4/e495be81f41917740b46b1bfd43fd5cde69041981463c3dbbd96e55d206a8806.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186416863 :question/problem "![](images/03/03ddf06402ad38708d232cf28783c817bf881965d54650eb158e7ffca4ea76a2.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent irreflexive relations defined on the set $A=\\{1,2,3\\}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the plots below represent irreflexive relations defined on the set $A=\\{1,2,3\\}?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186416863 :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nSince $R$ must be irreflexive, we require \n\n$$\n(1,1)\\notin R,\\,(2,2)\\notin R,\\,(3,3)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nThese ordered pairs correspond to the diagonal points on the plots, as shown below:\n\n![](images/b6/b63d99459e8271e70ec36fd417d67565f3d5e77316815d49c235468e174aa1e9.png)\n\n \n\nA plot corresponding to an irreflexive relation must not contain diagonal points. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nSince $R$ must be irreflexive, we require \n\n$$\n(1,1)\\notin R,\\,(2,2)\\notin R,\\,(3,3)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nThese ordered pairs correspond to the diagonal points on the plots, as shown below:\n\n![](images/b6/b63d99459e8271e70ec36fd417d67565f3d5e77316815d49c235468e174aa1e9.png)\n\n \n\nA plot corresponding to an irreflexive relation must not contain diagonal points. Therefore, the correct answer is plots I and III only"] [:db/cas 17592186473844 :answer-field/key "I" "![Plot I](images/a3/a3e03c2cbb421a0064d171bbe447257fe3888f6e5421500d596a5ad43ee50ed3.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186473845 :answer-field/key "II" "![Plot II](images/8d/8daa5270a726b99be5894c999a5828cc6c47ec0bc3498c228fde0f65e723f6ac.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186473846 :answer-field/key "III" "![Plot III](images/a0/a04b0baa453bf22462a75a55a8e6ec2678362bfacefaab62ae29a913dbaa940b.svg)"] [:db/cas 17592186417608 :question/problem "![](images/a8/a8ebf81e13d905d36997abf2f16c3732714b4feb56334f5e0c56443aa07732c6.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents an antisymmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the below digraphs represents an antisymmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186417608 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $3\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 3$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 2$ and $2\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $3\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 3$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 2$ and $2\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is digraph I only"] [:db/cas 17592186473859 :answer-fi